      "native_lines": [
        "MANNAN ",
        "Mannan (mannaan) is a tribe confined to Kerala. Major habitations of Mannans are ",
        "The speech of Mannans is closely related to Malayalam, and can be considered, a dialect of ",
        "terminations, but does not make use of the copula verb. Some lexical items of Mannan speech ",
        "The vowel phonemes are similar to those of Malayalam. Compared to Malayalam, Mannan ",
        "feature of Mannan phonology is the conversion of l of Malayalam and other S.Dr. languages ",
        "Mannan ",
        "-st- and –t- of Malayalam also have  c as their counter part in Mannan speech, in some words. ",
        "Malayalam  Mannan ",
        "rr becoming tt is another impotant phonological change attested in Mannan. ",
        "Malayalam  Mannan ",
        "s and h of Malayalam become k in Mannan. ",
        "Malayalam  Mannan "
      ],
      "inside_chapter": true
    },
    {
      "pdf_page": 46,
      "native_lines": [
        "Malayalam  Mannan ",
        "Malayalam  Mannan ",
        "Word final l of Malayalam is invariably dropped in Mannan. ",
        "Malayalam  Mannan ",
        "Malayalam  Mannan ",
        "The –laam plural suffix which occurs after kinship terms is a peculiar feature of Mannan "
      ],
      "inside_chapter": true
    },
    {
      "pdf_page": 47,
      "native_lines": [
        "Though Mannan employs the accusative suffix –e found in Malayalam also, more often ",
        "Malayalam. –nnu denoting present tense in Malayalam becomes –nu in Mannan. "
      ],
      "inside_chapter": true
    },
    {
      "pdf_page": 48,
      "native_lines": [
        "There is no copula verb in Mannan, unlike in Malayalam. ",
        "Like Malayalam, Mannan also does not have personal terminations.  ",
        "Mannan speech shows a large number of peculiar lexical items, not found in other languages "
      ],
      "inside_chapter": true
    },
    {
      "pdf_page": 49,
      "native_lines": [
        "Though most of the Mannans have adopted fashionable names of the plains people, a few "
      ],
      "inside_chapter": true
    },
    {
      "pdf_page": 50,
      "native_lines": [
        "are less in number compared to Malayalam. Like Mannan and some other tribal speeches, ",
        "continunant (l). Unlike Mannan, Muduga possesses voiced stops. "
      ],
